<sbgn xmlns="http://sbgn.org/libsbgn/0.3" xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0">
  <map id="QBM-01" language="process description">
    <extension>
      <qbo:mapNotes xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0">Mitochondrial electron transport and oxidative phosphorylation. The respiratory chain redrawn so that each node carries its quantum chemistry and the evidence behind it. Solid heavy borders mark entities where a magnetic-field effect has been demonstrated (T1); solid light, where it has been shown for the containing process (T2); dashed, where a physical route exists but no field measurement has been made (T3); hairline, structural context with no quantum claim (T4). The map is almost entirely dashed, and that is the finding: the Arabidopsis near-null-field literature is developmental and nutritional, and has not yet measured a respiratory complex directly. Alternative oxidase and the type II NAD(P)H dehydrogenases are drawn because they are the confounders — a plant can route electrons around a lesion in complex I or complex IV, so respiration rate alone cannot test the hypothesis and cytochrome-versus-alternative flux must be partitioned. Derived from: Figures 1A and 4 of Porterfield &amp; Barker, "Plant Responses to Near-Null Magnetic Fields" — expanded to gene level and bound to Arabidopsis loci</qbo:mapNotes>
    </extension>
