    <glyph id="COMPLEX_I" class="complex" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_inner_membrane">
      <label text="Complex I" />
      <bbox x="35.67" y="94.64" w="154.93" h="60.24" />
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_I__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="FMN" />
        <bbox x="41.67" y="85.64" w="30.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_I__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="8 × Fe/S" />
        <bbox x="41.67" y="85.64" w="56.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:COMPLEX_I" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_4Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_2Fe2S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>ros_leak_site</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>33S</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:58210</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G37510</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G08530</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G02580</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G11770</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005747</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">7.1.1.2</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>The FMN site forms a flavin semiquinone during turnover and is a known superoxide-producing leak site, and the Fe/S chain performs sequential one-electron transfers — both of the physical routes the radical-pair and hyperfine arguments require. No published experiment has applied a near-null field and measured plant complex I activity, so this is a hypothesis with a mechanism, not a result.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Electron transfer along the Fe/S chain is fast relative to plausible singlet-triplet mixing times, so a coherent radical-pair effect here is harder to argue than at the flavin. Treat the flavin and the cluster chain as separate hypotheses rather than one.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="COMPLEX_I_FMN" class="simple chemical" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_matrix">
      <label text="FMN site" />
      <bbox x="224.60" y="101.04" w="98.56" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_I_FMN__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="flavin semiquinone" />
        <bbox x="230.60" y="92.04" w="126.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:COMPLEX_I_FMN" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>radical_pair_acceptor</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>ros_leak_site</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:58210</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="chebi">CHEBI:58210</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G08530</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>A one-electron-reduced flavin is the best-characterised magnetosensitive species in biology; the same chemistry underlies the cryptochrome radical-pair model. The review's argument is precisely that this chemistry is not confined to photoreceptors.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="COMPLEX_II" class="complex" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_inner_membrane">
      <label text="Complex II (SDH)" />
      <bbox x="357.16" y="94.64" w="154.93" h="60.24" />
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_II__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="FAD" />
        <bbox x="363.16" y="85.64" w="30.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_II__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="2Fe-2S / 4Fe-4S / 3Fe-4S" />
        <bbox x="363.16" y="85.64" w="168.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:COMPLEX_II" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_2Fe2S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_4Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_3Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>ros_leak_site</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>33S</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:57692</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G66760</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT3G27380</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G40650</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G09600</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT2G46505</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005749</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">1.3.5.1</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>Carries all three cluster types plus a covalent flavin, and sits at the junction the review nominates as the bottleneck — a change here propagates into both TCA flux and respiratory electron flow.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="ALT_NADH_DH" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_inner_membrane">
      <label text="Type II NAD(P)H DH" />
      <bbox x="546.09" y="94.64" w="154.93" h="60.24" />
      <glyph id="ALT_NADH_DH__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="bypass — confounder" />
        <bbox x="552.09" y="85.64" w="133.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:ALT_NADH_DH" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:57692</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT1G07180</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT2G29990</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G28220</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G05020</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT2G20800</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>FAD-dependent, so the same flavin semiquinone chemistry applies. Included mainly as the second confounder alongside AOX — a plant can route around a complex I lesion, which blunts any phenotype and must be controlled for.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="UBIQUINONE_POOL" class="simple chemical" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_inner_membrane">
      <label text="Ubiquinone pool" />
      <bbox x="129.57" y="227.68" w="127.47" h="34.64" />
      <extension>
