    </glyph>
    <glyph id="RIESKE_FES" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_inner_membrane">
      <label text="Complex III Rieske centre" />
      <bbox x="97.25" y="335.12" w="187.50" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="RIESKE_FES__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="2Fe-2S" />
        <bbox x="103.25" y="326.12" w="42.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="RIESKE_FES__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="Q cycle" />
        <bbox x="103.25" y="326.12" w="49.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:RIESKE_FES" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>rieske_2Fe2S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>semiquinone_radical</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>ros_leak_site</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>33S</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G13430</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G13440</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005750</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>The Qo site generates a kinetically stabilised ubisemiquinone — a genuine, comparatively long-lived organic radical in a defined protein pocket, which is the most favourable setting for a field-dependent branching effect anywhere in the respiratory chain.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Qo-site semiquinone is short-lived and its occupancy is debated; a field effect here would be easier to argue if lifetime were measured under the relevant conditions.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="CYTOCHROME_C" class="macromolecule">
      <label text="Cytochrome c" />
      <bbox x="318.75" y="335.12" w="148.99" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="CYTOCHROME_C__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="c-type heme" />
        <bbox x="324.75" y="326.12" w="77.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:CYTOCHROME_C" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>heme_electron_carrier</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT1G22840</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G10040</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005758</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Racca2022" doi="10.1111/nph.18287" direction="altered">Cytochrome c and the transcription factor ABI4 link mitochondrial status to ABA-dependent seed germination.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>A one-electron heme carrier, paramagnetic in the ferric state. Included because Racca et al. place cytochrome c on the path from mitochondrial state to germination timing, which is the developmental window the review argues matters most.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="COMPLEX_IV" class="complex" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_inner_membrane">
      <label text="Complex IV (COX)" />
      <bbox x="501.74" y="341.52" w="137.70" h="34.64" />
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_IV__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="heme a3 / CuB" />
        <bbox x="507.74" y="332.52" w="91.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_IV__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="CuA" />
        <bbox x="507.74" y="332.52" w="30.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:COMPLEX_IV" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>heme_copper_binuclear</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>cu_a_binuclear</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>heme_electron_carrier</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005751</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">7.1.1.9</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>The review names cytochrome oxidase explicitly as a candidate direct metabolic target. The heme a3/CuB centre passes through paramagnetic oxygen intermediates (P, F, oxoferryl) whose spin states are, in principle, field-addressable.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Strongly exchange-coupled metal centres relax very fast, which argues against coherent spin effects. This is a mechanistically attractive but physically demanding hypothesis, and is labelled T3 for that reason.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="ATP_SYNTHASE" class="complex" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_inner_membrane">
      <label text="ATP synthase" />
      <bbox x="0.00" y="448.96" w="109.78" h="34.64" />
      <glyph id="ATP_SYNTHASE__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="readout: ATP/ADP" />
        <bbox x="6.00" y="439.96" w="112.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:ATP_SYNTHASE" evidenceTier="T4" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>structural_context</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005753</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">7.1.2.2</qbo:xref>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="MITO_SUPEROXIDE" class="simple chemical" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_matrix">
      <label text="Superoxide / H2O2" />
      <bbox x="143.78" y="442.56" w="143.93" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="MITO_SUPEROXIDE__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="signal amplifier" />
        <bbox x="149.78" y="433.56" w="112.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:MITO_SUPEROXIDE" evidenceTier="T2" confidence="reported">
          <qbo:quantumClass>ros_leak_site</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:xref db="chebi">CHEBI:18421</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="chebi">CHEBI:16240</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT3G10920</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Parmagnani2022" doi="10.3390/biom12121824" direction="altered" fieldRegime="NNMF">Near-null magnetic field exposure alters ROS-associated transcriptomic and metabolomic programmes in Arabidopsis.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Farooq2021" doi="10.3389/fpls.2021.781734" direction="altered">Reactive oxygen species and mitochondria jointly regulate seed germination, establishing the route from mitochondrial ROS to a developmental outcome.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>Not a magnetosensitive mechanism itself — it is the amplifier. A field effect too small to measure as a rate change can still register here, because ROS signalling is threshold-like.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="ACONITASE" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_matrix">
      <label text="Aconitase (ACO1/2/3)" />
      <bbox x="321.72" y="442.56" w="216.62" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="ACONITASE__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="4Fe-4S sentinel" />
        <bbox x="327.72" y="433.56" w="105.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:ACONITASE" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_4Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>33S</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G35830</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G26970</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT2G05710</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">4.2.1.3</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>The review nominates aconitase as the sentinel of Fe/S integrity, and the biochemistry supports it: the exposed cluster iron makes aconitase the first TCA activity to fall under oxidative stress, so it reports cluster damage before anything else does.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Precisely because aconitase responds to any oxidative insult, a change under NNMF would NOT by itself implicate a magnetic mechanism. It is a sensitive readout and a poor discriminator; it needs a paired ROS-independent control.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="LIPOYL_SYNTHASE" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_matrix">
      <label text="Lipoyl synthase (LIP1)" />
      <bbox x="572.34" y="442.56" w="164.35" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="LIPOYL_SYNTHASE__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="radical SAM" />
        <bbox x="578.34" y="433.56" w="77.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:LIPOYL_SYNTHASE" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_4Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>radical_pair_donor</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>33S</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT2G20860</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">2.8.1.8</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>A radical-SAM enzyme generating a 5'-deoxyadenosyl radical from a [4Fe-4S] cluster — genuine organic radical chemistry on an iron-sulfur centre, which is the tightest coupling of the review's two proposed mechanisms found anywhere in primary metabolism. Its product lipoylates pyruvate dehydrogenase and the 2-oxoglutarate dehydrogenase complex, so a defect propagates straight into TCA flux.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>The radical is generated inside the active site and quenched within one turnover; whether a spin-correlated pair with a long enough lifetime to be field-sensitive ever forms is untested.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
